{"id":"sw-10ef86","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-935f2cf3-571f-4a1b-99ad-2480029e833f","text":"## Summary\n\nWorkspace scaffold is in place on branch `sw-10ef86-scaffold-rust-workspace-engine-viewer-crates-ci`.\n\n- **Root `Cargo.toml`** — workspace of `crates/engine` + `crates/viewer`, `resolver = \"3\"`, edition 2024, `rust-version = \"1.94\"`, `publish = false`. `[workspace.lints]` (deny `unsafe_code`, warn `missing_docs`, `clippy::all`, `clippy::dbg_macro`) is inherited by both crates, so CI's `-D warnings` is meaningful. `[workspace.dependencies]` is the single place versions get pinned; it currently holds only the internal `smallworld-engine` path dep.\n- **`smallworld-engine`** (`crates/engine`) — `assets` and `shaders` modules plus `VERSION`. No third-party dependencies.\n- **`smallworld-viewer`** (`crates/viewer`) — binary named `smallworld`; prints engine version, host os/arch, resolved asset root and shader byte count, then exits. This is the CI smoke test until `sw-a2446a` replaces `main` with the window loop.\n- **Shader layout** — `crates/engine/shaders/*.wgsl`, baked in with `include_str!`, reached through `shaders::load(Shader::Common)`; `$SMALLWORLD_SHADER_DIR` swaps in on-disk files for rebuild-free iteration, and an unreadable override warns on stderr and falls back rather than failing. `shaders::compose(\u0026[..])` concatenates sources with `// ---- file ----` markers, since WGSL has no `#include`. `common.wgsl` carries `BRICK_EDGE`/`BRICK_VOLUME`/`VOXEL_SCALE` and `brick_index()` from DESIGN.md §3/D3.\n- **Asset convention** — `assets::root()` resolves `$SMALLWORLD_ASSETS` → `\u003cexe dir\u003e/assets` → `\u003cworkspace\u003e/assets`, cached in a `OnceLock`; `assets::path(\"a/b.png\")` joins component-wise so Windows needs no special case.\n- **`.github/workflows/ci.yml`** — `fmt` job plus a `check` matrix over ubuntu/macos/windows running clippy `-D warnings`, `cargo test`, and the viewer smoke test, with `Swatinem/rust-cache` and the Linux X11/Wayland/Vulkan apt step pre-installed for `sw-a2446a`.\n- **`.gitignore`** — `/target`, `**/*.rs.bk`, `.DS_Store`.\n\n## Rationale\n\nThe two conventions this story exists to fix (shaders, assets) are the ones later stories would otherwise each invent differently. Both are therefore *functions*, not constants at call sites, and both are unit-tested — including the Windows-relevant path joining, which the matrix now exercises on a real Windows runner.\n\nThe resolution rule in `assets::resolve` takes its inputs as parameters rather than reading the environment, because edition 2024 makes `env::set_var` `unsafe` (and racy across test threads) — with `unsafe_code = \"deny\"` the pure-function shape is required, not merely tidier. Same reason `shaders::read_from` takes a directory.\n\n## Decisions made\n\nBeyond the three confirmed in the spec (package naming, shader override, forward-loaded Linux CI deps):\n\n- **`publish = false`, no `license` field** — the project has no license chosen; asserting one in metadata would be inventing a fact. Revisit when a `LICENSE` file lands.\n- **`missing_docs = \"warn\"`** — with CI's `-D warnings` this makes doc comments mandatory on public engine API. Cheap now, expensive to retrofit later.\n- **`Cargo.lock` committed** — the workspace produces a binary.\n- **`Shader` is `#[non_exhaustive]`** — new passes get added every M0 story; downstream `match`es should not break.\n\n## Status\n\n`cargo fmt --all --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo build --workspace` all clean; `cargo test --workspace` passes 11 tests. The viewer was run from the workspace root, from an unrelated cwd, and under both env overrides — all four resolve correctly (see the walkthrough once approved for the captured output).\n\n**One acceptance criterion cannot be met locally:** the repo has no git remote, so `ci.yml` has never executed. It parses as valid YAML with the expected job/matrix/step structure and uses conventional actions, but \"CI is green\" is unverified until a remote exists. Flagged in the spec's Risks section rather than checked off.\n\nReady for review. Nothing is committed yet.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-05T20:08:45.037383Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-10ef86","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-08-05T20:24:44.852233Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-10ef86","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-bafda62a-09d5-4b4e-990d-c78e742ea0d5","text":"## Addendum — `Makefile` (review round 2)\n\nUser asked for a task runner. Spec updated first (new scope item 6, task-runner table, D9/D10), then implemented.\n\n```\nmake            → help (self-documenting, generated from ## comments)\nmake fmt        → cargo fmt --all                       (only target that edits files)\nmake lint       → cargo fmt --all --check + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\nmake test       → cargo test --workspace\nmake build      → cargo build --workspace               (RELEASE=1 adds --release)\nmake run        → cargo run -p smallworld-viewer        (RELEASE=1 adds --release)\nmake ci         → lint → test → build → run            (the ci.yml sequence)\nmake clean      → cargo clean\n```\n\n`CARGO ?= cargo` for toolchain substitution; `.NOTPARALLEL:` because every target contends for one cargo target directory. Written against GNU make 3.81 (stock macOS) — no make-4-only syntax.\n\n**Decisions:**\n\n- **`lint` includes the format check (D10).** The question being asked is \"will CI reject this?\", and formatting is one of the two ways it can. `fmt` stays the only file-modifying target.\n- **`ci.yml` still calls cargo directly, not `make` (D9).** GNU make is not guaranteed on the `windows-latest` runner image, and a task runner failing to *exist* is a confusing way to learn your build is fine. Cost is four duplicated command lines; both files now carry a comment pointing at the other.\n\n**Verified:** `make help/lint/test/build/run/ci` all behave, `RELEASE=1 make build` produces a release profile build, and `make ci` exits 0. Also confirmed the gate actually gates — with a deliberately misformatted function added to `lib.rs`, `make ci` stopped at `lint` with exit 2 rather than proceeding.\n\n`make ci` is the closest this repo can currently get to running its own workflow, given there is no remote (see the spec's Risks section).\n\n## Repo note\n\nReverting that probe edit with `git checkout -- crates/engine/src/lib.rs` **emptied the file** instead of restoring it: new files here are staged intent-to-add (`git add -N`), so their index blob is empty. `lib.rs` was rewritten byte-identically and everything re-verified green. Worth knowing before anyone reaches for `git checkout`/`git restore` on an uncommitted file in this repo — and `git add -A` is needed before a commit, since intent-to-add paths carry no content.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-05T20:28:11.664839Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-98a73d","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Remove jj colocation leftovers from the git repo","description":"Discovered while scaffolding the workspace (sw-10ef86): this repo was a **colocated jj repo**, snapshotted continuously by the VisualJJ VS Code extension's background server.\n\n**Why it matters.** jj auto-tracks new files and exports the working copy into the git index, so every uncommitted new file sits there as an *intent-to-add* entry — present in the index with an empty blob. `git checkout -- \u003cnew file\u003e` therefore does not revert the file, it **truncates it to zero bytes**. This cost us `crates/engine/src/lib.rs` during sw-10ef86 (rewritten from scratch, no lasting damage).\n\n**Decision (user, 2026-08-05):** remove jj from this repo entirely rather than tune `snapshot.auto-track`. The user is uninstalling the VisualJJ extension; `.jj/` is already gone.\n\n**Remaining cleanup**\n\n- [ ] Delete the orphaned `refs/jj/keep/*` refs left in `.git` — they pin jj's snapshot commits and keep their objects alive forever.\n- [ ] `git reset` (mixed — **never** `--hard`, there is uncommitted work) to drop the intent-to-add index entries, so new files show as plain `??` again.\n- [ ] `git gc --prune=now` to reclaim the orphaned snapshot objects.\n- [ ] Confirm no jj process is still running and `.jj/` does not reappear.\n- [ ] Verify branches (`main`, the sw-10ef86 story branch) and all uncommitted work survive intact — `make ci` green afterwards.\n\n**Not doing:** adding `.jj/` to `.gitignore`. The tool is being removed, not merely ignored.","status":"DOING","estimate":1,"sort_order":"a9","assignee":"Claude Code \u003cagent@MBPM1X.local\u003e","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-98a73d","target_id":"sw-10ef86","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["improvement","infra"]},"created_at":"2026-08-05T20:37:31.340501Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
