{"id":"sw-00ae86","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-642402e8-f39b-495e-806a-ca57e5cf99c6","text":"## Summary\n\nProcedural terrain generator using 3D density + noise functions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `crates/engine/src/worldgen.rs` — `WorldGenerator` with self-contained hash-based value noise + FBM. 3D density terrain (overhangs, caves), depth-based strata (grass → dirt → stone → dark stone), water table fills depressions. 4 new unit tests.\n\n**Modified files:**\n- `crates/engine/src/lib.rs` — registered module\n- `crates/viewer/src/main.rs` — replaced `generate_test_world()` with `generate_world()` using `WorldGenerator`. Grid 16×10×16, camera at (0, 8, 14).\n- `Cargo.toml` — `profile.dev.package.smallworld-engine` opt-level 3 (noise math is unusable without optimization)\n- `Makefile` — added `screenshot` target for window-only captures\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **opt-level 3 for engine in dev builds.** The noise functions are ~20x slower without optimization. 550ms worldgen vs 10+ seconds. This is the standard Rust approach for crates with hot math.\n- **Self-contained noise, no `noise` crate.** ~60 lines of hash-based value noise + FBM. Zero dependencies, fast, deterministic.\n- **Smaller grid (16×10×16) for fast debug iteration.** Can be scaled up with release builds or when streaming is implemented.\n\n## Status\n\n25 tests pass, clippy clean. World generates in ~550ms with strata, caves, and water. Ready for review."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T00:12:08.222759Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
