{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-9368a248-ccdf-407a-944a-74dc2b80281a","text":"## Summary\n\nTwo-phase world construction + streaming, per spec:\n\n- `coarse_svo.rs` (new, sandbox) — threaded `find_surface_y` heightfield, min/max pyramid, recursive air/solid/crossing build with strata + water colors. **1 km world: 0.35 s, 3.63M nodes, 2.44M streaming candidates** (AC budget: 5 s / 8M pool).\n- `svo.rs` — `alloc_child`/`set_color` construction API, `remove_brick` (sw-fcea39), path-local ancestor color refresh in `insert_brick`, dirty-range tracking + `upload_dirty` (coalesced ranged writes), coarse-solid subdivision materializes all 8 children (prevents air holes when bricks stream into buried cells).\n- `raymarch.wgsl` — `trace_svo` restructured to cursor-based descent: one stack frame per level (`MAX_SVO_DEPTH = 16`). The old push-all-children stack (24 entries) needs up to 7 × depth live entries and silently dropped subtrees at depth 10.\n- `brick_pager.rs` — per-column candidate bands replace the dense cell grid, HashMap states for touched cells only, O(1) counter stats, eviction detaches the SVO brick handle.\n- `gpu_worldgen.rs` — `generate_cells(positions)` replaces `generate_near` box scan; priming targets exactly the preload's candidate cells.\n- `scenes.rs`/`main.rs` — Large World preset (1638.4 m, [1024,16,1024], 8M-node SVO, 60 m preload, 8 workers, flythrough); Default/TerrainOnly share the two-phase path.\n\n## Rationale\n\nDistant terrain needs only tree structure + colors, both derivable from surface heights — no voxel data, no pool slots. Interior-node cubes ARE the LOD representation, so a complete coarse tree makes the whole 1 km world visible before a single brick exists.\n\n## Decisions made (beyond spec, recorded there)\n\n- Unweighted child-color averaging (was: weighted by descendant count) — consistent between full and incremental passes without per-node subtree counts; visually negligible difference.\n- No separate pager-level async eviction test — same code path as the SVO-level test plus bookkeeping; async timing would make it flaky. Large World flythrough exercises it end-to-end.\n- Headless shader-validation test added (`raymarch_shader_validates`) — WGSL breakage now fails `cargo test` instead of first app launch.\n\n## Status\n\n38 tests pass (incl. ignored 1 km timing test run explicitly), clippy zero warnings, fmt clean, release build ready. Manual checks remaining: Default visual regression, Large World flythrough ≥ 30 fps + streaming/eviction behavior."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T16:11:22.130847Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-fcea39","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-63bfd6c7-5d0c-462c-b5a6-bbd489de7b10","text":"Fixed as part of sw-f089de: `Svo::remove_brick` clears flag bit 8 and the handle while keeping the leaf's averaged color as LOD fallback; `BrickPager::drain_results` calls it before `pool.reassign` on every eviction. Regression test `remove_brick_keeps_color_clears_handle` covers the round-trip. End-to-end eviction behavior is exercised by the Large World flythrough (demand ~30k candidates vs 32k pool slots cycles the LRU)."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T16:11:24.318763Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T18:50:46.266834Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-7530e437-4373-4cf8-9b7c-e708d1897821","text":"## Review round 1: floating coarse boxes (\"terraced slabs\")\n\n**Root cause** — `find_surface_y` scans only terrain density, but `sample()` also carves caves. Where a cave breaches the surface, worldgen returns air for cells the coarse pass painted grass. Streaming marked them `Air` but never removed the coarse color, leaving opaque green slabs floating over the real terrain (1394 Air cells on Default: ~1024 expected above-surface pad cells + ~370 cave breaches — the terraces in the screenshot).\n\n**Fix**\n- `Svo::clear_leaf` — full clear (color + brick) when a streamed candidate comes back empty; descends with allocation so cells inside coarse solid nodes subdivide-then-clear.\n- Dynamic band extension — air at a column band's bottom extends the band one cell down and requests it (cave floors live below the heightfield estimate). Terminates at solid ground or the world floor.\n- `recompute_color` zeroes nodes whose children are all invisible (stale opaque ancestors otherwise still rendered at SSE-coarse distances).\n\n**Test** — `preload_leaves_no_floating_boxes`: full Default preload through a CPU source, then asserts every column's topmost visible cell actually contains solid voxels per the generator. Verified the test catches the bug: with `clear_leaf` neutered it flags the exact terrace runs from the screenshot ((0..3, y=7), (11..14, y=8), ...).\n\nFull suite green (32 engine + 8 sandbox), clippy zero warnings, release build ready for re-test."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T18:50:57.105013Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:02:32.974233Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-e643aab2-4fa4-4b54-8615-50633cbd8cfd","text":"## Review round 2: flat gray cave walls/floors\n\n**Root cause** — round 1 correctly cleared cave-air cells, which opened up cavities visually, but the cavity's *walls, floor, and ceilings* are solid cells outside every column's heightfield band: they never streamed and rendered as flat coarse stone. The downward-only band extension covered floors directly under a column, not laterally exposed faces.\n\n**Fix** — `extend_bands_around_air` replaces `extend_band_below`: every discovered air cell pulls all 6 neighbors into the candidate set (contiguous band growth). The flood is bounded by the cavity surface — solid results stop it. During `preload_radius`, extension requests are capped to the preload radius (world-spanning cave systems can't stall startup); outside it cells stay Unknown for demand streaming.\n\n**Test strengthened first (fails on old code)** — `preload_leaves_no_floating_boxes` now builds ground-truth occupancy for every cell (threaded `generate_brick` pass) and asserts two invariants after full preload: (1) no opaque cell where the generator says air, (2) **every solid cell with an air neighbor carries brick detail** — rule 2 flagged the exact gray walls from the screenshot before the fix, zero after.\n\nFull suite green, clippy zero, release rebuilt. Ready for round-3 visual check."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:02:42.799398Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-d8e0d5","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Region cache: unbounded open files kill pager workers; no cache versioning","description":"Found during sw-f089de round 3. User-reported panics:\n\n```\nthread 'brick-pager-5' panicked at cached_source.rs:46: failed to open region file: Os { code: 24, \"Too many open files\" }\nthread 'brick-pager-4' panicked at cached_source.rs:36: PoisonError { .. }\n```\n\n**Bug 1 — fd exhaustion:** `CachedSource` keeps every opened `RegionFile` in its map forever. Regions are 16³ cells, so the Large World preset has 4096 region files; macOS's default fd limit is 256. When the limit hits, `open_or_create` panics (`expect`), poisoning the regions `RwLock` — every other worker then panics on `read().unwrap()`. With all workers dead, in-flight cells never resolve and exposed terrain stays coarse (the varying flat-gray patches across rounds).\n\n**Bug 2 — no cache versioning:** region files persist across code changes and mix GPU-shader results, old-run results, and CPU-fallback results with no version stamp. Any historical divergence between those sources is frozen into the cache and re-served forever.\n\n**Fix:** LRU cap on concurrently open region files (evicted entries close on last Arc drop, safe for in-flight readers); open failure degrades to the inner source instead of panicking; poison-proof lock access; a `VERSION` stamp in the region dir that wipes the cache on mismatch, bumped now to invalidate all existing caches.","status":"DOING","sort_order":"b0Y","assignee":"Claude Code \u003cagent@macbook.local\u003e","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-d8e0d5","target_id":"sw-f089de","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","sandbox"]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:06:49.638713Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-c9d281","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"GPU worldgen shader diverges from CPU generator (7/12288 cells)","description":"Found during sw-f089de round 3 via the new `gpu_matches_cpu_worldgen` parity test: the WGSL worldgen and the CPU `WorldGenerator` produce bit-different voxels on 7 of 12288 Default cells (first: cell [21,2,9], both solid but different content). Likely FMA contraction in the GPU's fbm evaluation flipping material/air decisions at thresholds — which is exactly where cave surfaces live.\n\n**Why it matters:** the pager mixes GPU-primed cells and CPU-fallback cells in a single run, so divergent cells produce self-inconsistent geometry at source boundaries (flat faces where one source's air borders the other source's solid).\n\n**Mitigated already** (sw-d8e0d5 round): GPU results are now persisted to the region cache with first-write-wins, so a generated world is at least *stable* across runs.\n\n**Proper fix options:**\n1. Make the WGSL fbm bit-exact with CPU (eliminate FMA contraction, match operation order) and keep this parity test green as the contract.\n2. Drop GPU priming and generate everything on CPU workers — release-mode CPU gen measured fast enough (~12k bricks \u003c 1 s threaded); removes the divergence class entirely at the cost of the GPU path.\n\nThe parity test is checked in but `#[ignore]`d until this is resolved — run with `cargo test gpu_matches_cpu -- --ignored`.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"b0X","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-c9d281","target_id":"sw-f089de","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"sw-c9d281","target_id":"sw-d8e0d5","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","sandbox","worldgen"]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:09:47.65014Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-d8e0d5","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-97420361-8858-4953-a897-84aa00990bc0","text":"## Summary\n\n- `CachedSource` now caps concurrently open region files at 64 with oldest-first eviction (evicted files close when their last in-flight `Arc` drops). Regression test `open_regions_stay_capped` touches 3× the cap and asserts the map never exceeds it.\n- Region open failure degrades to the inner source (`log::warn` + bypass) instead of panicking a worker; all region-map locks are poison-proof (`unwrap_or_else(PoisonError::into_inner)`). A cache can no longer take down the streaming pipeline.\n- Added `CACHE_VERSION` stamp (bumped to 2): mismatched region dirs are wiped on startup, invalidating every cache written by older code/generators. Test `version_mismatch_wipes_cache` covers wipe + keep paths.\n- `GpuCachedSource` now persists GPU-primed results into the region cache (first-write-wins via new `CachedSource::store`), so worlds stay bit-stable across runs despite the GPU/CPU generator divergence tracked in sw-c9d281.\n\n## Status\n\nAll tests green, clippy zero, release built. The user-visible failure this fixes: dead pager workers silently starving exposed terrain of bricks (the varying flat-gray patches in sw-f089de rounds 2–3)."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:12:03.367094Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-a9e303ae-ca2c-4d5a-8644-7d6e0048a023","text":"## Review round 3: waterline walls — root cause found via headless pixel probe\n\n**Diagnosis path** — CPU-source test pager end state matched the app HUD exactly (resident 2045 / air 4658), proving app data == test data, so the flat gray had to be *representable in the verified data*. A new headless render probe (`render_default_headless`, ignored diagnostic — renders the Default world with the user's exact camera to a PPM) reproduced the artifact pixel-for-pixel without the app.\n\n**Root cause** — exposure was modeled cell-granularly everywhere: the streaming flood and the test invariant both treated any non-empty brick as \"solid, stops the flood.\" But a **partial brick exposes its neighbors through its own air voxels**. Canonical case: the cell straddling the water level (water voxels below, air above) hid the wall cell behind it from the candidate flood — while visually revealing that wall's coarse face through its air half. 1182 cells were in this class on Default (the flat gray walls at/above the waterline in every screenshot).\n\n**Fix** — the candidate flood now extends around any result containing air: fully-air cells AND partial bricks; only fully solid bricks stop it (`BrickPager::is_full`). Default resident 2045 → 3227.\n\n**Verification** — invariant test strengthened first to voxel-granular exposure (fails with 1182 violations on old code, zero after); headless probe re-render shows fully detailed walls to the waterline. Suite green, clippy zero, release built."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:34:24.022358Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-2ef59d","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"GPU timestamp queries report identical values for all passes","description":"Across every session today the HUD shows near-identical numbers for all three passes — e.g. Compute 80.34 / Blit 80.47 / egui 80.44 ms (also 17.35/17.50/17.47 and 28.78/28.94/28.90 earlier). Three different workloads reporting the same duration to within 0.2 ms means the timestamp queries are almost certainly measuring the same interval (shared query indices, or begin/end pairs resolving against the wrong slots) rather than per-pass time. `GpuTimestamps` in `gpu_timing.rs`. Until fixed, per-pass GPU numbers are not usable for the sw-2230ee perf work — only GPU Total (and even that is suspect).","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"b0Y","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-2ef59d","target_id":"sw-2230ee","kind":"dependency_of"}],"labels":["bug","engine"]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:39:38.687341Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f089de","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-152e2d3f-90be-455e-bbf6-fdf352a8d0af","text":"## Large World first run (user-verified)\n\nThe 1 km world builds, renders, and streams: 21,231 resident bricks / 80,669 air / 2.4M untouched candidates, CPU frame 2.1 ms. Construction and streaming machinery is working as designed.\n\n**Performance AC not met in this story:** GPU compute reads ~80 ms on the flythrough viewpoint (HUD timestamps are suspect per sw-1c9e-timestamps issue, but the magnitude is real). Moving the ≥30 fps criterion to sw-2230ee (1 km validation at target spec), where it belongs with the tuning knobs:\n\n- `trace_shadow` runs a full closest-hit `trace_svo` per shadow ray; the spec's planned any-hit early-exit was never implemented — likely the single largest win.\n- Demand radius is a fixed 50 cells (80 m): mid-distance terrain (80–300 m) renders coarse cells even where SSE wants bricks. Radius/pool/SSE-threshold tuning is sw-2230ee scope.\n- Visible coarse-cell contour terracing at distance is the LOD-quality gap: needs mid-distance brick coverage plus transition treatment (dither/blend) to hit the \"unnoticeable LOD\" epic goal."},"created_at":"2026-08-06T19:39:44.502512Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.223 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
