{"id":"sw-ce99bd","type":"DELETE","payload":{},"created_at":"2026-08-07T07:38:26.680723Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"web"}
{"id":"sw-449c64","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## OOC Pipeline Stage: Resolve\n\nThe trait seams that every pipeline stage programs against. `VoxelVolume` exposes enough structure for shader-level traversal optimization via a `VolumeKind` discriminant. AABB and depth buffers get their own high-performance data structures, separate from volume data, so culling can run ahead of shading.\n\n### Scope\n- ECS A/B spike (bevy_ecs vs hecs)\n- `VoxelVolume` trait with `VolumeKind` enum for shader dispatch\n- AABB buffer abstraction for culling\n- Depth buffer abstraction decoupled from render targets\n- World skeleton (lightweight spatial hierarchy)\n- `Renderer` and `MeshExtractor` trait definitions\n\n### Phase\nPhase 1 — pipeline skeleton runs end-to-end","labels":["epic","engine","ooc-pipeline","abstractions","phase-1"],"sort_order":"b0O","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E1: Core Abstractions \u0026 ECS"},"created_at":"2026-08-07T08:24:30.067591Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-764a6a","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## OOC Pipeline Stage: Resolve\n\nAt least one `VoxelVolume` must be real for the pipeline to run. We start by wrapping the existing SVO, then add ChunkedDDA and FlatGrid. Each populates the shared AABB buffer. The thread pool and data sources (worldgen, disk cache) are what keep volumes filled — they're inseparable from this stage.\n\n### Scope\n- `SvoVolume` wrapping existing SVO+BrickPool\n- `ChunkedVolume` (32³ brick chunks, BVH macro skip, DDA micro)\n- `FlatGrid` volume (uniform 3D array, DDA)\n- Generalized `BvhAccel\u003cT: Bounded\u003e`\n- Extended brick data (Hermite intersections + normals)\n- Split thread pool (I/O vs compute)\n- Data source integration (BrickSource, GPU/CPU worldgen, disk cache)\n- GPU benchmark harness\n\n### Phase\nPhase 1 — pipeline skeleton runs end-to-end\n\n### Dependencies\n- Depends on E1 for trait definitions","labels":["epic","engine","ooc-pipeline","volumes","streaming","phase-1"],"sort_order":"b0P","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E2: Volume Implementations \u0026 Data Sources"},"created_at":"2026-08-07T08:24:43.817397Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-2e26ca","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## OOC Pipeline Stage: Cull\n\nThis stage decides what the rest of the pipeline works on. Without it, we stream and render everything within radius regardless of visibility. Frustum culling, hierarchical-Z occlusion, and SSE evaluation all run as GPU compute — they read the AABB buffer and depth buffer from Stage 1 and output a visibility set that drives Stage 3 (streaming) and Stage 4 (execution).\n\n### Scope\n- GPU frustum culler (AABB vs frustum planes)\n- Hierarchical Z-Buffer construction (mip-chain depth pyramid)\n- GPU occlusion culler (AABB vs HzB)\n- GPU SSE evaluator (replaces CPU-side BrickPager::classify_demand)\n- Visibility readback and streaming feedback\n\n### Phase\nPhase 2 — real GPU-driven culling replaces \"render everything in radius\"\n\n### Dependencies\n- Depends on E1 for AABB buffer and depth buffer abstractions\n- Depends on E2 for volume data populating those buffers","labels":["epic","engine","ooc-pipeline","culling","gpu-compute","phase-2"],"sort_order":"b0Q","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E3: Culling Pipeline"},"created_at":"2026-08-07T08:24:54.175135Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e74f7a","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## OOC Pipeline Stage: Stream\n\nMoves data from the resolve stage (CPU/disk) to GPU memory without stalling the frame. The ring buffer pattern is the core mechanism: workers write into a cyclic staging buffer, a copy queue transfers to VRAM, fences gate recycling.\n\n**Metal path:** cyclic `MTLBuffer` sized for N frames in flight, `MTLBlitCommandEncoder` for copies, `dispatch_semaphore` for frame-in-flight gating.\n\n**Vulkan path:** dedicated async transfer queue where available.\n\n### Scope\n- Ring buffer allocator (cyclic upload heap)\n- Metal copy path (MTLBuffer + MTLBlitCommandEncoder + semaphore)\n- Vulkan async transfer path (dedicated queue + fences)\n- Priority queue with cancellation tokens\n- VRAM/RAM budget controller with LRU eviction\n\n### Phase\nPhase 2 — ring buffer + copy queue streaming replaces per-brick writes\n\n### Dependencies\n- Depends on E2 for volume data to stream\n- Depends on E3 for visibility-driven load prioritization","labels":["epic","engine","ooc-pipeline","streaming","gpu-transfer","phase-2"],"sort_order":"b0R","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E4: Streaming \u0026 Allocation"},"created_at":"2026-08-07T08:25:04.503159Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-5304ba","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## OOC Pipeline Stage: Execute\n\nThe first execution path. Refactor the existing raymarcher behind the `Renderer` trait so it can be composed with the rasterizer. It consumes whatever `VoxelVolume` implementations are resident, dispatching optimized traversal kernels per `VolumeKind`. This is mostly refactoring — the raytracer already works.\n\n### Scope\n- Refactor `Raymarcher` behind `Renderer` trait\n- Wire as default OOC execution stage\n- Volume-type shader variants (SVO, ChunkedDDA, FlatGrid dispatch)\n\n### Phase\nPhase 1 — ships with E1+E2 to keep the engine rendering throughout\n\n### Dependencies\n- Depends on E1 for Renderer trait and VoxelVolume trait","labels":["epic","engine","ooc-pipeline","raytracing","rendering","phase-1"],"sort_order":"b0S","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E5: Execution — Raytracing"},"created_at":"2026-08-07T08:25:14.283543Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a5e257","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## OOC Pipeline Stage: Execute\n\nThe second execution path. Extract meshes from volumes, decompose into meshlets with LOD DAGs, rasterize via GPU-driven indirect draws. The compositor merges rasterizer output (near terrain, objects) with raytracer output (GI, shadows, distance) into a unified frame. This is where the hybrid engine comes together.\n\n### Scope\n- Marching Cubes extractor\n- Dual Contouring extractor (requires Hermite brick data from E2)\n- Meshlet decomposition (meshoptimizer first, own Nanite if churn)\n- GPU meshlet SSE evaluator\n- LOD transitions (geomorphing, dithered blending)\n- Chunk boundary stitching (Transvoxel / skirts)\n- Rasterizer implementing `Renderer` trait\n- Compositor pass (merge raytrace + rasterize)\n- Pipeline mode switching (raytrace-only, rasterize-only, hybrid)\n\n### Phase\nPhase 3 — second execution path, hybrid engine\n\n### Dependencies\n- Depends on E1 for Renderer and MeshExtractor traits\n- Depends on E5 for G-buffer format compatibility\n- Depends on E2 for extended brick data (Hermite)","labels":["epic","engine","ooc-pipeline","meshing","rasterization","rendering","phase-3"],"sort_order":"b0T","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E6: Execution — Meshing \u0026 Rasterization"},"created_at":"2026-08-07T08:25:25.843955Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
