{"id":"sw-a6dea7","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-a6dea7","target_id":"sw-dcc28a"}],"description":"## GPU Cluster Assignment\n\nMove light-to-cluster assignment from CPU to a GPU compute pass. CPU assignment works fine for \u003c100 lights but becomes a bottleneck at 1000+.\n\n### Deliverables\n\n- Compute shader that reads the light SSBO and writes cluster assignments\n- Replace CPU cluster loop with a single dispatch\n- Benchmark: CPU vs GPU assignment at 100, 500, 1000 lights\n\n### When to do this\n\nAfter the CPU-side clustered lighting (sw-dcc28a) is validated and working. This is a pure optimization — the interface stays the same.","estimate":3,"labels":["optimization","engine","rendering","lighting"],"parent_id":"sw-fb5789","sort_order":"b15","status":"BACKLOG","title":"GPU-side clustered light assignment"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T08:36:38.041309Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.226 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-f967e8","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-f967e8","target_id":"sw-dcc28a"}],"description":"## Multi-Cascade Shadow Maps\n\nExtend the single-cascade directional shadow (sw-dcc28a) to 3-4 cascades for large outdoor scenes. Single cascade covers ~50m; cascaded shadow maps are required for open-world rendering where shadows need to reach hundreds of metres.\n\n### Deliverables\n\n- 3-4 cascade splits (configurable) with stable cascade boundaries\n- Per-cascade atlas region in the existing shadow atlas\n- Cascade selection in shade pass based on fragment depth\n- Smooth cascade blending at boundaries (avoid visible seams)\n- Cascade split scheme: practical split (logarithmic/uniform blend, tunable lambda)\n\n### Design note\n\nThis builds on the shadow atlas and shadow view infrastructure from sw-dcc28a. The atlas subdivision, shadow pipeline, and shade pass shadow sampling all need to support multiple views per light.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","engine","rendering","shadows"],"parent_id":"sw-fb5789","sort_order":"b16","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Multi-cascade CSM for directional shadows"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T08:36:45.315954Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.226 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a08abf","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-a08abf","target_id":"sw-dcc28a"}],"description":"## Soft Shadow Filtering\n\nReplace single-tap hard shadows (sw-dcc28a) with filtered shadow sampling for soft shadow edges.\n\n### Deliverables\n\n- **PCF (Percentage-Closer Filtering):** 3×3 or 5×5 kernel with rotated Poisson disk sampling\n- **PCSS (Percentage-Closer Soft Shadows):** contact-hardening shadows where penumbra width varies with blocker distance\n- Quality setting: hard (1 tap) → medium (PCF) → high (PCSS)\n- Shadow map filtering compatible with the shadow atlas (handle atlas seams)\n\n### Design note\n\nPCF is the minimum for shipping quality. PCSS gives physically-motivated soft shadows but costs more taps. Both should be configurable at runtime.","estimate":3,"labels":["feature","engine","rendering","shadows"],"parent_id":"sw-fb5789","sort_order":"b17","status":"BACKLOG","title":"PCF / PCSS soft shadows"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T08:36:50.876533Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.226 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-dcc28a","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"action":"updated","artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T08:43:30.43146Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.226 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-0dd1c0","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-0dd1c0","target_id":"sw-dcc28a"}],"description":"## Virtual Shadow Maps\n\nReplace the fixed shadow atlas with UE5-style virtual shadow maps: 16k×16k virtual resolution per light, 128×128 pages allocated on demand from depth buffer analysis.\n\n### Deliverables\n\n- Virtual shadow map abstraction: 16k×16k virtual texture per shadow-casting light\n- Page table: maps virtual pages to physical page pool slots\n- Page allocation: analyze depth buffer to determine which virtual pages need rendering\n- Physical page pool: configurable GPU memory budget, pages cached and invalidated per-frame\n- Mip chain for distance-based page selection (far geometry gets coarser pages)\n- Point lights: 6-face cubemap of VSMs\n- Integration with existing shade pass shadow sampling\n\n### Why\n\nVirtual shadow maps eliminate the fixed atlas sizing problem entirely. Instead of subdividing a 4096² atlas across N lights (each getting progressively lower resolution), every light gets consistent 16k virtual resolution with only visible pages actually rendered. This is especially important for:\n\n- Large outdoor scenes with long shadow draw distances\n- Many shadow-casting lights (torches, campfires, etc.)\n- Close-up shadow detail without wasting resolution on distant shadows\n\n### Design note\n\nThis is a significant architectural step up from the grid-subdivided atlas (sw-dcc28a). The atlas subdivision interface should be designed with VSM as the eventual replacement in mind — the shade pass shadow sampling should abstract over \"give me shadow at this world position\" regardless of whether the backing is an atlas region or a virtual page.\n\n### References\n\n- [UE5 Virtual Shadow Maps docs](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/virtual-shadow-maps-in-unreal-engine)\n- `notes/research/shadow-atlas-sizing.md` for comparison data","estimate":8,"labels":["feature","engine","rendering","shadows","optimization"],"parent_id":"sw-fb5789","sort_order":"b18","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Virtual shadow maps (VSM) with page-based allocation"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T08:44:45.495735Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.226 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-713dd5","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Advanced Rendering Tech\n\nRendering quality and performance improvements beyond the basic working pipeline (E1.5). These are follow-ups discovered during implementation of the core pipeline stages — each one is independently valuable and moves the engine closer to modern rendering tech.\n\n### Categories\n\n**Shadows**\n- Multi-cascade CSM for large outdoor scenes\n- PCF / PCSS soft shadow filtering\n- Virtual shadow maps (UE5-style page-based allocation)\n\n**Lighting**\n- GPU-side clustered light assignment (scales to 1000+ lights)\n\n**Geometry**\n- Skinned mesh support (bone weights, skeleton, animation)\n\n### Relationship to E1.5\n\nE1.5 gets the pipeline working end-to-end with correct output. E7 pushes each subsystem toward production quality. E1.5 issues are prerequisites — E7 work depends on the basic infrastructure being in place.","estimate":8,"labels":["epic","engine","rendering","optimization"],"sort_order":"b19","status":"BACKLOG","title":"E7: Advanced Rendering Tech"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T08:48:42.058391Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.226 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
