{"id":"sw-2c3e44","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"## Job System\n\nTwo-layer architecture for parallel work execution: a swappable **Scheduler** backend\n(thread pool + work distribution) and a stable **Task API** (dependencies, parallel-for,\nserial pipes, cancellation).\n\n### Architecture (from engine research)\n\nBased on comparative analysis of UE5, Unity, and Godot 4 job systems\n(see `docs/research/job-system-comparison.md`):\n\n- **Two-layer design** (UE5) — scheduler and task API are separate. Lets us start with\n  rayon and swap to a custom work-stealing scheduler later without changing the API.\n- **Work-stealing** (UE5/Unity consensus) — Chase-Lev deques, LIFO locally (cache-warm),\n  FIFO stealing (coarse-grained). Godot's centralized FIFO has known scaling issues at 24+ cores.\n- **Atomic prerequisite counting** (UE5) — decentralized dependency resolution, no global lock.\n- **Task retraction** (UE5) — when waiting on an unstarted task, retract and execute inline.\n  Prevents deadlock in a fixed-size pool.\n- **Three priority levels** with promotion (Godot) — urgent/normal/background. Low-priority\n  tasks promote when slots open, preventing starvation.\n- **Serial pipes** (UE5 FPipe) — sequential execution without a dedicated thread or mutex.\n- **Rust's borrow checker replaces runtime safety** — no need for Unity's AtomicSafetyHandle\n  or Godot's debug-only thread guards. `Send + 'static` on tasks is sufficient.\n\n### Scope\n\n- `Scheduler` trait with rayon backend (phase 1), custom crossbeam backend (phase 2)\n- `TaskHandle` with atomic dependency counting and task retraction on wait\n- `parallel_for(range, batch_size, fn)` — the bread-and-butter primitive for voxel work\n- Three priority levels: urgent (physics, audio), normal (streaming, meshing), background (saves, prefetch)\n- Serial pipes for ordered execution (GPU commands, asset loading)\n- Cooperative cancellation tokens\n\n### Thread Layout\n\n```\nMain thread          — game logic, input, frame orchestration\nGPU submission       — dedicated, receives command buffers via channel\nAudio thread         — dedicated, lock-free ring buffer feed\nWorker pool (N-3)    — work-stealing, handles all parallel work:\n                       streaming, meshing, culling, LOD, physics,\n                       scripting, worldgen, saves\n```\n\n### Non-goals (game crate territory)\n\n- Specific job orchestration (worldgen pipeline, save serialization)\n- AI behavior scheduling\n- Script execution scheduling (Scripting Runtime epic)\n\n### Existing Code\n\nThe persistent worker pool from sw-b87f8e (`JobPool`) is the current baseline.\nStory 1 wraps/replaces it behind the `Scheduler` trait.\n\n### Research\n\n- `docs/research/job-system-comparison.md` — synthesis and recommendations\n- `docs/research/ue5-task-system.md` — UE5 architecture\n- `docs/research/unity-job-system.md` — Unity architecture\n- `docs/research/godot4-threading-architecture.md` — Godot 4 architecture"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:23:55.218253Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e475fe","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Scheduler trait + rayon backend","description":"## Scheduler Trait + Rayon Backend\n\nDefine the two-layer architecture: a `Scheduler` trait (swappable backend) and a stable\ntask API surface. Initial backend is rayon.\n\n### Scope\n\n- **`Scheduler` trait** — `spawn(task)`, `parallel_for(range, batch_size, fn)`, `shutdown()`\n- **`TaskHandle`** — opaque handle returned by `spawn()`. Supports `wait()`, `is_complete()`.\n- **Rayon backend** — wraps `rayon::ThreadPool` behind the `Scheduler` trait.\n  Thread count: `cores - 3` (reserving main, GPU submission, audio).\n- **`parallel_for`** — splits a range into batches, dispatches to pool. Configurable\n  `batch_size` parameter. Batch size guidance: trivial work (vector add) → 32-256,\n  expensive work (meshing) → 1-4.\n- **Wrap/replace `JobPool`** — the existing persistent worker pool from sw-b87f8e is\n  absorbed behind the `Scheduler` trait. Callers migrate to the new API.\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `Scheduler` trait defined with `spawn`, `parallel_for`, `shutdown`\n- [ ] `RayonScheduler` implements `Scheduler` using `rayon::ThreadPool`\n- [ ] `TaskHandle` returned by `spawn()` with `wait()` and `is_complete()`\n- [ ] `parallel_for` distributes work across pool with configurable batch size\n- [ ] Existing `JobPool` callers migrated to new API\n- [ ] Benchmark: `parallel_for` over 10K items shows linear scaling to available cores\n- [ ] Unit tests for spawn, parallel_for, wait, shutdown","estimate":3,"labels":["feature","engine","jobs"]},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:24:08.838868Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-ac1601","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Task dependencies with atomic prerequisite counting","description":"## Task Dependencies\n\nAtomic prerequisite counting (UE5 pattern) for expressing task ordering. Each task tracks\nits own outstanding prerequisite count — fully decentralized, no global scheduler lock.\n\n### Scope\n\n- **Atomic prerequisite counter** — `AtomicU32` per task. Predecessors decrement on\n  completion; zero triggers queueing. `fetch_sub(1, Release)` + `fence(Acquire)` on zero.\n- **Dependency API** — `spawn_after(task, \u0026[TaskHandle])` schedules a task that waits for\n  all prerequisites to complete before queueing.\n- **`join(handles)`** — combine multiple `TaskHandle`s into a single handle that completes\n  when all inputs complete.\n- **Task retraction on wait** — when `TaskHandle::wait()` is called and the target task\n  hasn't started, retract it from the queue and execute inline on the calling thread.\n  Prevents deadlock when all workers are blocked waiting. Recursive retraction of\n  prerequisites.\n- **Main-thread result channel** — bounded `crossbeam-channel` for posting results back to\n  the main thread. `Scheduler::drain_results()` processes completed result callbacks.\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `spawn_after(task, \u0026[TaskHandle])` queues task only when all prerequisites complete\n- [ ] Atomic prerequisite decrement — no global lock on the critical path\n- [ ] `join()` combines multiple handles into one\n- [ ] Task retraction: waiting on an unstarted task executes it inline\n- [ ] No deadlock with N workers all waiting on each other's tasks (retraction resolves it)\n- [ ] Result callback channel delivers to main thread\n- [ ] Unit tests for dependency chains, diamond dependencies, retraction","labels":["feature","engine","jobs"]},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:24:19.43066Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a93f4d","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"[Retired] Job pool priority queues with work-stealing","description":"**Retired** — absorbed into the reworked Job System epic. Priority levels are now story sw-e475fe successor (story 3), work-stealing is covered by the custom scheduler story (story 6). See sw-2c3e44 for the updated breakdown.","status":"DONE"},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:24:26.498824Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-232d69","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Priority levels with starvation prevention","description":"## Priority Levels with Starvation Prevention\n\nThree-tier priority system with low-priority promotion (Godot pattern). Prevents starvation\nwithout complex priority queues.\n\n### Scope\n\n- **Three priority levels:**\n  - **Urgent** — physics, audio feed, frame-critical work. Always dequeued first.\n  - **Normal** — streaming, meshing, culling, LOD. Default priority.\n  - **Background** — compaction, saves, worldgen prefetch. Runs when urgent/normal are idle.\n- **Priority parameter** on `spawn()` and `parallel_for()` — `TaskPriority::Urgent | Normal | Background`\n- **Starvation prevention** — when a normal/urgent task completes and a background slot\n  opens, promote one background task to the active queue. Prevents indefinite starvation of\n  low-priority work.\n- **Background thread cap** — at most `pool_size / 2` threads run background tasks\n  simultaneously. Reserves capacity for urgent work arriving mid-frame.\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `TaskPriority` enum with Urgent, Normal, Background\n- [ ] `spawn()` and `parallel_for()` accept optional priority parameter (default: Normal)\n- [ ] Urgent tasks preempt normal/background in dequeue order\n- [ ] Background tasks are promoted when slots open — no indefinite starvation\n- [ ] Background thread cap prevents background work from consuming the entire pool\n- [ ] Benchmark: urgent task latency unaffected by background load","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"sw-2c3e44","estimate":3,"sort_order":"b1I","labels":["feature","engine","jobs"]},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:24:36.61089Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-39f679","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Serial pipes for ordered execution","description":"## Serial Pipes\n\nSequential execution channels (inspired by UE5 FPipe). Tasks on the same pipe execute\nFIFO but can migrate between worker threads. Gives sequential semantics without a dedicated\nthread or mutex — no priority inversion, no deadlock risk.\n\n### Scope\n\n- **`Pipe` struct** — lightweight, created via `Scheduler::create_pipe()`\n- **`pipe.spawn(task)`** — enqueues a task that will execute after all prior tasks on this\n  pipe complete. The task runs on any available worker thread.\n- **FIFO ordering** — tasks on the same pipe execute in submission order\n- **No dedicated thread** — pipe tasks run on pool workers, migrating as needed\n- **`pipe.is_empty()`** and **`pipe.wait_until_empty()`** for synchronization\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- GPU command buffer recording — serialized access without a mutex\n- Asset loading queues — ordered processing of load requests\n- Per-entity scripting — script ticks for a single entity run sequentially\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `Pipe` created via `Scheduler::create_pipe()`\n- [ ] Tasks on the same pipe execute in FIFO order\n- [ ] Tasks on different pipes execute concurrently\n- [ ] `wait_until_empty()` blocks until all pipe tasks complete\n- [ ] No mutex, no priority inversion\n- [ ] Unit tests for ordering guarantees, concurrent pipes, wait semantics","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"sw-2c3e44","estimate":2,"sort_order":"b1J","labels":["feature","engine","jobs"]},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:24:45.851965Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-120de1","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Cooperative cancellation tokens","description":"## Cooperative Cancellation Tokens\n\nCancel tokens for long-running jobs. Checked cooperatively inside task loops — the task\ndecides when to check and how to clean up.\n\n### Scope\n\n- **`CancelToken`** — shared atomic flag. `is_cancelled()` returns bool. Cheap to check\n  (single atomic load, `Relaxed` ordering sufficient).\n- **`CancelSource`** — owns the token, provides `cancel()` to set the flag. Dropping the\n  source does NOT auto-cancel (explicit cancellation only).\n- **Integration with `spawn()`** — `spawn_cancellable(task, \u0026CancelToken)` passes the\n  token to the task closure. Task checks `token.is_cancelled()` in its loop.\n- **Integration with `parallel_for()`** — `parallel_for_cancellable(range, batch_size, token, fn)`\n  checks cancellation between batches. Already-running batches complete; pending batches\n  are skipped.\n- **No preemption** — cancellation is cooperative, not forced. Tasks must check the token.\n  This is deliberate: forced cancellation leaves resources in inconsistent states.\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Streaming: cancel pending brick loads when the camera teleports\n- LOD: cancel in-progress mesh extraction when the chunk leaves view\n- Worldgen: cancel prefetch when the player changes direction\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `CancelToken` + `CancelSource` with `is_cancelled()` / `cancel()`\n- [ ] `spawn_cancellable()` passes token to task\n- [ ] `parallel_for_cancellable()` skips pending batches on cancel\n- [ ] Cancellation check is a single atomic load (\u003c 1ns)\n- [ ] Unit tests for cancel-before-start, cancel-mid-execution, cancel-parallel-for","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"sw-2c3e44","estimate":2,"sort_order":"b1K","labels":["feature","engine","jobs"]},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:24:55.84623Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-2ca320","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Custom work-stealing scheduler on crossbeam-deque","description":"## Custom Work-Stealing Scheduler\n\nReplace the rayon backend with a custom `Scheduler` implementation built on `crossbeam-deque`.\nThis is the \"we outgrew rayon\" optimization — same task API, better control over priority,\nretraction, and wake-up.\n\n### Motivation\n\nRayon is great for prototyping but lacks: priority levels (everything is equal), task\nretraction at the scheduler level, serial pipes as a first-class concept, per-task\ncancellation, and fine-grained wake-up control. A custom scheduler gives us all of these\nwhile keeping the `Scheduler` trait API stable.\n\n### Scope\n\n- **Chase-Lev work-stealing deques** — one per worker thread via `crossbeam-deque`.\n  LIFO locally (cache-warm), FIFO stealing (coarse-grained).\n- **Per-thread futex wake-up** — `parking_lot::Condvar` per worker (or raw futex on Linux,\n  `os_unfair_lock` on macOS). O(1) wake cost regardless of pool size. No global semaphore.\n- **Scheduler-level task retraction** — when `wait()` targets an unstarted task, retract it\n  from the deque and execute inline. Recursive retraction of prerequisites.\n- **Priority-aware dequeue** — urgent deque checked before normal, normal before background.\n  Three deques per worker thread.\n- **Oversubscription detection** — when all workers are blocked on `wait()`, activate a\n  standby thread (UE5 5.5 pattern). Parks when a blocked worker resumes.\n- **Task state machine** — bit-flag atomic CAS transitions (Ready → Scheduled → Running →\n  Completed). Lock-free lifecycle management.\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `CrossbeamScheduler` implements `Scheduler` trait — drop-in replacement for rayon\n- [ ] All existing tests pass with the new backend\n- [ ] Work-stealing measured: \u003c 5% idle time under uniform load\n- [ ] Per-thread wake-up: O(1) cost verified on 16+ core machine\n- [ ] Task retraction prevents deadlock (same test as story 2)\n- [ ] Priority ordering: urgent tasks complete before normal under contention\n- [ ] Benchmark: ≥ rayon throughput on parallel_for, lower latency on spawn+wait\n\n### Dependencies\n\n- Depends on all prior Job System stories (implements the same trait)\n\n### Research\n\n- `docs/research/ue5-task-system.md` — Chase-Lev, task retraction, oversubscription\n- `docs/research/job-system-comparison.md` — build-vs-buy analysis","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"sw-2c3e44","estimate":5,"sort_order":"b1L","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-2ca320","target_id":"sw-e475fe","kind":"depends_on"},{"source_id":"sw-2ca320","target_id":"sw-ac1601","kind":"depends_on"}],"labels":["optimization","engine","jobs"]},"created_at":"2026-08-08T12:25:12.44899Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.225 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
